# {{name}}

Rosette photonic design project. Python scripts in `designs/` produce GDSII layout files.

## ALWAYS read the reference files before coding

Before writing or modifying any Rosette code, read these files:

- `.rosette/api.pyi` -- public Python API reference for agents
- `.rosette/cli.json` -- complete CLI contract (every command, its flags, exit codes, and `--json` schema), discoverable without prose. Equivalent to `rosette cli-manifest`.
- `.rosette/manifest.json` -- installed package version and managed-reference provenance
- `rosette.toml` -- project config, layer definitions (named layers), DRC rules, DFM config
- `components/__init__.py` and relevant `components/*.py` modules -- the template-specific component catalog, source, signatures, and documentation (the blank template starts with no public components)

Your training data may be outdated -- `.rosette/` matches the installed core package, while `components/` is the authoritative project-local component API.

`.rosette/` is gitignored and may be absent after a fresh clone. If a reference is missing, run `uv run rosette update`; do not rerun `rosette init` in an existing project.

## ALWAYS build then check

A design is not complete until it passes checks. After every build, run checks and fix any violations before presenting the result:

```
uv run rosette build designs/foo.py
uv run rosette check designs/foo.py --json
```

Always pass `--json`: checks then emit a single JSON object on stdout — read `passed` directly and use each violation's `bbox` (in microns) to locate the problem, instead of parsing prose that may reword. A passing build only means the GDS was written -- it does NOT mean the design is correct. `rosette check` catches DRC violations (min width, spacing, overlap), unconnected ports, width mismatches, and bend radius violations. Run `rosette drc`/`rosette dfm` (both support `--json`) to check those in isolation; `rosette dfm` predicts as-manufactured geometry and is also folded into `rosette check --include-dfm`.

## Key conventions

- Designs must export a `design` variable (a `Cell` instance)
- Use explicit imports (`from rosette import Cell, Layer`; `from rosette.project import load_layer_map`; `from components import my_component`) rather than star imports
- Use `load_layer_map()` from `rosette.project` to get layers from rosette.toml -- never hardcode layer numbers
- The package is `librosette` (installed via `uv add librosette`, imported as `rosette`)
- To inspect a design visually, `uv run rosette shot designs/foo.py` renders a PNG (plus a sidecar JSON with the world<->pixel transform) to `.rosette/snapshots/`

